    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate is headlined by **Tyler Soderstrom** and **Mickey Moniak**, our clear-cut priority starts with efficiency scores of 76.91 and 75.75, respectively. Both benefit immensely from facing Lowder and Martinez, whose elevated xERAs over 4.80 provide a target-rich environment. **Mickey Moniak** is particularly intriguing; his projection is boosted significantly by a +14% park factor and a favorable platoon matchup, while **Tyler Soderstrom** enjoys a +18% lift from the SP\u2019s poor command profile. We are also deploying **Seiya Suzuki** (73.90) in the #2 hole against Lauer; while facing a same-handed pitcher is usually a hurdle, Lauer\u2019s 5.20 xERA is too exploitable to ignore, making this our most efficient path to securing Runs and RBI.\n\nThe strategy today leans into volume against vulnerable arms, but we remain vigilant regarding the SB scarcity mandate. **Corbin Carroll** (67.36) remains a high-floor play despite a -4% penalty for the opposing catcher\u2019s elite hold percentage; his elite speed keeps his value insulated. The toughest call on the bench was **Cole Young**, who landed at a 37.96 score, just missing our 40.0 floor. While his #1 batting order slot provided a +10% cushion, the overwhelming negative impact of facing Anderson\u2019s 3.15 xERA and a poor park factor left **Cole Young** on the outside looking in. Similarly, **Zach McKinstry** failed to crack the lineup despite a slight boost from a slow-moving pitcher, as his #9 spot in the order hampered his upside. We continue to monitor the status of **James Wood** and **Jose Caballero**, but with neither available, we are relying on our depth to carry the day. Tread carefully with **Brice Turang** and **Austin Riley**; while they are in the lineup, they are facing stiff competition against Skenes and P\u00e9rez, respectively, and are strictly rotational plays until they demonstrate more consistency against top-tier pitching.",
